---
// The second tool page. One URL, indexable, its own title and prose — people arrive here
// from a search for "rotate pdf", not from the landing page.
//
// The interactive part is one island. Everything above and below it is static, so the page is
// readable and its claim is checkable with JavaScript switched off.
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Rotate PDF — turn pages on your own computer | Not Only PDF"
  description="Turn pages that were scanned the wrong way up. The pages are rotated in your browser and never uploaded, because there is no server to upload them to."
>
  <div class="read">
    <h1>Rotate PDF</h1>

    <p>
      Turn some or all of a PDF's pages a quarter or a half turn — the usual fix for a scan that
      came out sideways. The rotating happens in this browser, on this computer: the file is not
      uploaded, because Not Only PDF has no server that could receive it.
    </p>
  </div>

  {
    /*
    NO `client:*` DIRECTIVE. Astro's hydration directives bootstrap an island with an INLINE
    script, and `script-src 'self'` carries no `'unsafe-inline'` and no nonce (ADR 0014) — so
    the browser refuses the bootstrap, the island never hydrates, and the tool renders as dead
    markup with nothing visible but a CSP line in the console. Measured on `/merge-pdf`; the
    same wall, the same answer. The component is mounted by the module below, which Astro
    bundles to `_astro/*.js` and references with a `src`.

    The ENGINES are still not loaded here. The tool starts a worker the first time a file is
    chosen (ADR 0018). Mounting the component is a few kilobytes; starting the engines is
    about 420 KB over the wire (1.8 MB uncompressed) --- it was 6.8 MB until spike 0004 took
    PDFium out of the payload.
  */
  }
  <div id="rotate-tool"></div>

  <script>
    import { type Component, mount } from "svelte";

    import RotateTool from "../components/RotateTool.svelte";

    const target = document.getElementById("rotate-tool");
    if (target) {
      // The cast is Astro's type, not ours: `@astrojs/svelte` declares a `.svelte` default
      // export as an ASTRO component, because that is what a `client:*` directive would need.
      // Mounting it by hand is the same module reached through Svelte's own API, and the two
      // declarations do not overlap. See the comment above on why `mount` is the only correct
      // call here.
      mount(RotateTool as unknown as Component, { target });
    }
  </script>

  <noscript>
    <div class="read no-js">
      <h2>This tool needs JavaScript</h2>
      <p>
        The rotating is done by code running in your browser, so with JavaScript switched off there
        is nothing to do the work. There is no server-side version to fall back to — that is the
        whole design, not a missing feature.
      </p>
      <p>
        With JavaScript on, this page lets you choose a PDF, pick which pages to turn and which way,
        and download the result. Nothing is sent anywhere.
      </p>
    </div>
  </noscript>

  <div class="read">
    <h2>Choosing pages by number</h2>

    <p>
      You can turn every page, or type the ones you want: <code>3</code>, or <code>2-5</code>, or
      <code>1, 4, 7-9</code>. Not Only PDF shows how many pages the document has once it has opened
      it.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Pages are shown as pictures once you choose a file.</strong> They are drawn on your computer
      by the same code that does the rotating, and each one is drawn when you scroll to it rather than
      all at once. A page too complex to draw quickly says so and is skipped; the selection still works
      by number, which is how this page worked before there were pictures at all.
    </p>

    <details>
      <summary><h2>What happens to large files</h2></summary>

      <p>
        Everything is held in this browser tab's memory, so the practical limit is what your device
        can spare rather than an upload size. Not Only PDF refuses a file larger than 512 MB before
        it opens it, and a document of more than 10,000 pages before it turns any of them — so an
        oversized job is refused rather than failing part way through and leaving you with something
        incomplete.
      </p>

      <p>
        What that does not save you from, said here rather than discovered: a very large document
        that is under both limits can still make the tab slow while it runs, and on a phone the
        browser may stop the tab entirely. That is the device's memory ceiling, reached inside the
        PDF engine where nothing can intervene.
      </p>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary><h2>What is kept, and what is not</h2></summary>

      <p>
        <strong>Everything.</strong> Rotating changes one attribute on the pages you chose and touches
        nothing else: bookmarks, attachments, form fields, annotations and the page contents all come
        through unchanged. The pages themselves are not re-encoded, so a scan is not resampled and a photograph
        does not lose quality — the document simply records that those pages display turned.
      </p>

      <p>
        That is worth stating plainly because the other tools here cannot say it. <a
          href="/merge-pdf">Merging</a
        > keeps bookmarks from the first file only; a tool that takes pages out of a document has to drop
        what pointed at the pages it left behind. Rotating drops nothing, and Not Only PDF's tests check
        the claim rather than assuming it: every bookmark, annotation, form field and attachment the document
        carried must still be there afterwards, and each page's drawing instructions must come through
        unaltered.
      </p>

      <p>
        A file that is encrypted, damaged, or not a PDF is refused. So is one where a page you asked
        to turn already carries a rotation that is not a whole number of quarter turns — Not Only
        PDF will not guess at what was meant, because guessing would turn a page on a value the file
        never carried. A page you did not ask for is never read, so a document can carry an odd
        rotation elsewhere and still be usable here.
      </p>
    </details>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* The no-JS block is ordinary content, but it only ever appears in place of the tool, so it
     gets the same top margin the tool has. */
  .no-js {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-6);
  }
</style>
